From: OpenIRD contributors
Subject: [PATCH] mips: allow FPU opcodes in the FPU context switch code

The kernel is compiled -msoft-float, and gcc hands that on to the
assembler.  Since binutils 2.25 a soft-float assembly rejects the FPU
load/store instructions, so the two files that save and restore the FPU
registers no longer assemble with the pinned binutils 2.29.1:

  arch/mips/kernel/r4k_fpu.S:59: Error: opcode not supported on this
      processor: mips3 (mips3) `sdc1 $f0,272+0($4)'

Those instructions are the whole point of these files.  Say so with
".set hardfloat", which only widens what the assembler accepts from that
line on; it does not change the object's float ABI or anything gcc
generates.  (This SoC has no FPU; the code is reached only through the
emulator's lazy-FPU paths, but it has to build.)

Mainline later did the same, behind a toolchain test.  The toolchain is
pinned here, so the directive is used unconditionally.

Origin: gpl-derived
---
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/r4k_fpu.S
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/r4k_fpu.S
@@ -31,6 +31,9 @@
 
 	.set	noreorder
 	.set	mips3
+	/* The kernel is built -msoft-float; binutils 2.25 and later then
+	 * refuse FPU load/store opcodes unless told otherwise. */
+	.set	hardfloat
 
 LEAF(_save_fp_context)
 	cfc1	t1, fcr31
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/r4k_switch.S
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/r4k_switch.S
@@ -23,6 +23,11 @@
 
 #include <asm/asmmacro.h>
 
+	/* The kernel is built -msoft-float; binutils 2.25 and later then
+	 * refuse the FPU load/store opcodes in fpu_save_double and
+	 * fpu_restore_double unless told otherwise. */
+	.set	hardfloat
+
 /*
  * Offset to the current process status flags, the first 32 bytes of the
  * stack are not used.
